#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198130 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/id #uuid "a77db107-d157-5063-b44a-1ca0a93be099" :question/math-academy-id "e-12951" :question/problem "![](images/ae/aec65d50659286de9cbf0e03639e0dd1cbaa24307339398d47a825deed89a8d9.png)\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$\n\n- $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthogonal\n\n- $\\{\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1),\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)\\}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}.$\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $|| \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) || = || \\mathbf{x} ||.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n![](images/bf/bfc573de171024e15387f4c3b67d68d7423860c150f4453938e535a0ad05352f.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)\\cdot\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2) = \\mathbf{u}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{u}_2 = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, both $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{u}_2)$ are unit vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q({u}_{1})‖ & =‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3})^{2}}=1 \\\\ ‖Q({u}_{2})‖ & =‖{u}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{(-\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198131 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/id #uuid "0070a453-0237-571f-84fc-a9f58446e6a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12954" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{Q}$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. If ![](images/86/86bb7df4c111a6fd93c1891d3fceafdbcbe2c49dd80a9e91f623f7e97120d7d0.png) and ![](images/ac/ac195edae526d86fdfdab05c1b118b14bd1756397027bec2ad360e0491d987e0.png), find\n\n- $\\Vert \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\Vert.$\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have that $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}.$\n\nThe linear transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $\\mathbf{Q}$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $|| \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) || = || \\mathbf{x} ||.$\n\n- If $\\mathbf{x} \\perp \\mathbf{y}$ then $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\perp \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y})$ for any $\\mathbf{x}, \\mathbf{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n.$ In general, $\\mathbf{Q}$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $\\mathbf{Q}$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $\\mathbf{Q}$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $\\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{x}) \\cdot \\mathbf{Q}(\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{x} \\cdot \\mathbf{y}.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186446169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd4d334-2269-5bfe-a24c-a58aafbef588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446156} {:answer/id #uuid "4a46d5a3-3c48-5050-85ff-c52a5b9baaee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446157} {:answer/id #uuid "9164dcc8-fbe9-5d34-a2f1-c8be97a45615" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446159} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c1505c-5ad9-511e-a22d-30a3286bfbdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446160} {:answer/id #uuid "e705ebf2-c558-54c0-816f-602c5af2facd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e705ebf2-c558-54c0-816f-602c5af2facd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446163} :answer-field/id #uuid "45554d58-8407-5972-941e-77d5ad8113b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186446166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bffc4e85-37a7-58e6-9707-94d9ac87c744" :question/math-academy-id "q-177893" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81d08864-6101-5251-8351-5c0043998d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472778} {:answer/id #uuid "1a22dbe5-0d2b-5c0a-9531-8c54bafdc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a22dbe5-0d2b-5c0a-9531-8c54bafdc81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472779} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e4259d-b2ae-5c52-927d-7cf47899f776" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473784} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84ccb50-f1be-591e-8bac-35014caba949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472780} {:answer/id #uuid "19621db5-93e2-50ee-9ddb-8440f2d6f313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19621db5-93e2-50ee-9ddb-8440f2d6f313" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472781} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af95f9f-6948-54ed-b3d3-37b3b7e7a68c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473785} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b309452-f7e7-54e9-bbdf-9a3025bd85c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472782} {:answer/id #uuid "f4968354-f03a-5157-9f0d-8be8dbd50643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b309452-f7e7-54e9-bbdf-9a3025bd85c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472782} :answer-field/id #uuid "46245871-924f-5edd-8bb1-a6c28bc755b4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-178014" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411865 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f8e2907-6f9f-5945-87a4-4b320b3c5c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472784} {:answer/id #uuid "363acb19-a7c4-58f9-889f-e407b2331d8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f8e2907-6f9f-5945-87a4-4b320b3c5c7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472784} :answer-field/id #uuid "51607494-5a75-5d4c-92f2-cb8a34490a90" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473787} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30463986-efbd-51fd-a6e9-b681bf1c31ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472786} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0850f2-9a46-5214-af3b-d2ee5b9d05f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30463986-efbd-51fd-a6e9-b681bf1c31ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472786} :answer-field/id #uuid "576b8513-a337-57bb-a0b8-42a8d11e9c97" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2da457c8-29f9-53a4-9a7b-82bd8117f5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472788} {:answer/id #uuid "9252ae02-4d55-5e0b-ab06-d7222907f63c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da457c8-29f9-53a4-9a7b-82bd8117f5ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472788} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8436333-b398-5406-b728-7406ac2b71f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d807e12-6960-57f6-b74d-aa3fe66f0f76" :question/math-academy-id "q-178271" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0$\n\nII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthogonal\n\nIII. $\\{Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})\\}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=3\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{3})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{3})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406641} {:answer/id #uuid "7c3a74ea-6a6d-5da3-b53b-157fcb966ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406643} {:answer/id #uuid "b81c5bc9-bfe5-5ce5-928d-fa684e207472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406645} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a8591a-2779-5016-a0d3-d51746099b13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406647} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f62bc1-8a13-5efa-ad9a-759018432b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33d47db5-c434-55d6-9456-167ab8a0b67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406641} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac075b6f-3299-5310-ad52-26f9cf648385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf616da5-d6d0-5837-a9fb-64c2cf137040" :question/math-academy-id "q-40488" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "047c19ef-c76a-57b3-b13e-81cac8438947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473112} {:answer/id #uuid "0f411e21-0bfc-5e60-be10-aa9385c60d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f411e21-0bfc-5e60-be10-aa9385c60d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473113} :answer-field/id #uuid "41b5e6b2-6fa4-5699-91d3-98491ea01274" :answer-field/key "U" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473951} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b5e99f8-6d7b-5605-8eeb-768c689a1a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} {:answer/id #uuid "d3e8b7c2-03e8-52a8-8c30-1f1597b0a406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b5e99f8-6d7b-5605-8eeb-768c689a1a11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2909a2f7-876c-5e10-a289-6837f83679fc" :answer-field/key "V" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829a1b56-ccd5-5381-aeca-6ba71098f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473116} {:answer/id #uuid "adcc7d05-7e81-525a-b650-a03adf317913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adcc7d05-7e81-525a-b650-a03adf317913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473117} :answer-field/id #uuid "51b28bdf-55b7-5470-b1f6-bd8182431d5f" :answer-field/key "W" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" :question/math-academy-id "q-40491" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n- **U.** $U={Q([\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$\n- **W.** $W={Q([\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])}$\n- **V.** $V={Q([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]),Q([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}])}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198448 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "455b3713-34a3-535d-8baf-d279a0341e9f" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Angle Preserving Property of Orthogonal Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0303d738-8cc7-59ec-8b7d-200d38ab8c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324000} {:answer/id #uuid "82d1627d-ec51-5470-8fc5-d8eafe211195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325071} {:answer/id #uuid "a16383c9-5611-572c-b608-547d4f386c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325339} {:answer/id #uuid "c0c18e5c-2129-5c21-b1de-4402da5c72ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325628} {:answer/id #uuid "d03fd62d-486d-5ae9-9fce-4399e51612b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325783}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a16383c9-5611-572c-b608-547d4f386c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325339} :answer-field/id #uuid "93cce25a-ee90-5496-ba71-5859157a921f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326482}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9841eea4-968b-5da0-9aa5-7188bb572485" :question/math-academy-id "q-40513" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-4)+(-1)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
